            {
              "id": "83516454-8972-3d39-b930-f68873f94032",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions during the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed for the standard memory copy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, both the source and destination pointers may be null at this point in the code, and there are no explicit checks to prevent this. The absence of a guard to ensure valid pointers and non-overlapping regions further increases the risk. The verdict is supported by the high-confidence classification and the lack of mitigating evidence.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use a memory move function that is designed to handle overlapping regions. Additionally, check that both source and destination pointers are not null before performing the operation.\n\nExample remediation:\n\n```cpp\nif (workspace_proposals.dptr_ != nullptr && &anchors[0] != nullptr) {\n    // Ensure regions do not overlap before copying\n    if (workspace_proposals.dptr_ + (sizeof(float) * anchors.size()) <= (float*)&anchors[0] ||\n        (float*)&anchors[0] + anchors.size() <= workspace_proposals.dptr_) {\n        std::memcpy(workspace_proposals.dptr_, &anchors[0], sizeof(float) * anchors.size());\n    } else {\n        // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n        std::memmove(workspace_proposals.dptr_, &anchors[0], sizeof(float) * anchors.size());\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis approach checks for null pointers and ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before using the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, it uses a memory move function that safely handles overlapping regions.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b826b485-98e5-3eee-8fbc-10dd0c83df59",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Unsecured Web Address\" vulnerability refers to the use of unencrypted HTTP protocol to communicate sensitive information over the internet. This can lead to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks where an attacker can intercept and possibly alter the communication between two parties without their knowledge. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a programmer uses an unsecured HTTP URL (like `http://www.64lines.com`) to send or receive sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use HTTPS (HTTP Secure) instead of HTTP when dealing with sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data between your browser and the website that you are connected to. This means that even if someone manages to intercept the data, they would not be able to understand it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsecured HTTP URL with a secured HTTPS URL. If the website supports HTTPS, simply change `http://` to `https://`.\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nstd::string url = \"http://www.64lines.com\";\n\n// Fixed code\nstd::string url = \"https://www.64lines.com\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
