              "id": "bc4dea93-f575-3aeb-856f-29187e0aa7e3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator. This can be particularly dangerous in scenarios where the random number generator is used for security-critical operations such as generating encryption keys, random identifiers, etc.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `std::shuffle` is used with a potentially weak random number generator `rnd_`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::shuffle(inst_order_.begin(), inst_order_.end(), rnd_)\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved using the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators including some that are cryptographically secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937` which are cryptographically secure:\n\n```cpp\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\nstd::shuffle(inst_order_.begin(), inst_order_.end(), g);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<algorithm>` for `std::shuffle`\n- `<random>` for `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
