            {
              "id": "171690a6-fe72-35c7-a2b9-58ee3fdff188",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `strcmp()` function is used, which can lead to potential security risks. The `strcmp()` function does not perform any bounds checking and can therefore lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking. For instance, instead of `strcmp()`, use `strncmp()`. Always validate and sanitize input data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcmp()` function with `strncmp()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nif (strncmp(key, \"quality\", sizeof(\"quality\") - 1) == 0)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
