              "id": "1a111c61-8d11-34a0-877d-db19a27c7e58",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the function `strcmp()` is used, which can lead to potential security risks if not used properly. The `strcmp()` function compares two strings and can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the strings are not properly null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `strcmp()`. In C++, the `std::string` class should be used for string manipulation, which provides safer methods for string comparison.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcmp()` function with the `std::string` comparison. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string key_str(key);\nif (key_str == \"pack_label\") {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library is required for the `strcmp()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
