              "id": "2256fb21-9500-38ee-990e-44f9117a5302",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that are prone to errors, which can lead to serious security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `strcmp()` function is used, which can lead to potential buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the language. For instance, in C++, you can use `std::string` and its member functions to compare strings, which is safer than using `strcmp()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcmp()` function with the `==` operator for `std::string`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string key = /* some value */;\nif (key == \"center_crop\") {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
