              "id": "30c7f191-3e00-3637-9e4e-7777a4c100b1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `strcmp()` function is used, which can lead to potential security risks if not used properly. The `strcmp()` function does not check for null bytes and can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For instance, instead of `strcmp()`, use `std::string::compare()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcmp()` function with `std::string::compare()`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string key_str(key);\nif (!key_str.compare(\"label_width\")) {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strcmp()`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
